WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.940
Adventures of Tom Sawyer.

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:05.220
Join Tom on a journey of action-packed adventure.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:27.860
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.540
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:30.980
And I'm Joe.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:35.780
The old lady pulled her spectacles down and looked over them about the room.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.600
Then she put them up and looked out under them.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:43.700
She seldom or never looked through them for a so small a thing as a boy.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:50.140
They were her state pair, the pride of her heart, and were built for style, not service.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:54.280
She could have seen them through a pair of stove lids just as well.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:01:00.860
She looked perplexed for a moment and then said, not fiercely, but still loud enough for the furniture to hear.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:06.820
And that is the first chapter of Tom Sawyer, the book by Mark Twain.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:12.180
Twain gang. Did that end on, like, she said and then not finishing what she said?

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.840
I think it was like a she said, like, to end a sentence.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:17.960
Oh, okay. You know.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.040
I guess I drifted out of that for a little bit.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:27.200
Yeah. I'm not sure, like, what, like, what that means either at the end, though.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.460
Like, loud enough for the furniture to hear.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.600
But, you know, furniture doesn't have ears.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:34.080
So I guess that's like, you know, crazy book talk.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:35.600
Yeah, literature is bullshit.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.660
But really, though, what is the appeal of these games?

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.080
Is the appeal of games like this and Dr.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:50.960
Jackal and Mr. Hyde supposed to, like, beat a fool parents into thinking that, like, now their kids can experience acclaimed literature, like, in a new format?

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.000
Like, do they think that they're just getting the Sparknotes video game version?

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.000
That's sort of what I'm getting the feel for.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:07.880
Like, I, you know, for some reason, Tom Sawyer and Huck Finn was not a part of our syllabus in middle or high school.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.460
So I never actually read these books.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.440
I just, like, read about Mark Twain.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:15.120
It was like, oh, yeah, he's, he did stuff.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.060
So I really don't know what any of this is based off.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:20.380
It could just be all bullshit.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.760
And, like, he just wears a hat.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.360
So therefore, he's Tom Sawyer.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:30.360
But yeah, I guess I'm not the target demographic or ever was.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:34.820
Yeah, I also never read, never read this in school or anything.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:37.800
So, so, so I'm not super clear on it all either.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:44.420
I didn't think it was, I didn't think just from my, my, like, general knowledge that it was like a fantastical.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.600
Story, unless it is about his, like, imagination or whatever.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:53.420
I don't know. I don't want to make myself stupid by talking about Tom Sawyer and not knowing what I'm talking about.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:55.300
But, but I don't know.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.400
I, I, I was thinking the same thing.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:02.280
I was wondering, like, why would they choose, like, literary characters for their video games?

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:09.340
And I mean, another option could be that maybe it's just like, okay, here's a generation of people who don't know Tom Sawyer.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.680
We don't have to create a character.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.420
We already have some elements.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.080
We can just, we can just do it again.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.100
I mean, I think that works more with, like, movies.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:23.580
But, like, if you think about something like, like Sherlock Holmes, like, they're still, you know, they're still making Sherlock Holmes stuff now and, like, introducing a new generation to it.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:30.980
Not that I think that the Adventures of Tom Sawyer for the NES was, like, a great way to introduce a new generation to the material.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:39.020
Yeah, we need, like, a dark and gritty reboot about Tom Sawyer, like, the imaginations of, like, Gen Z kids or something.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:52.740
Right, right. And the Adventures of Tom Sawyer, the video game, is not really, like, the book other than you're Tom Sawyer and I guess, like, you're still, um, I forget the name of the girl.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.180
Somebody who read the manual would remember.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:55.380
Becky. Becky something.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:57.460
Okay, yeah, you're going to save her.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:02.340
So, like. So, that's the historic love interest of Tom Sawyer.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:04.520
Well, it's Tom Sawyer and Huckleberry Finn, right?

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:08.120
Which, technically, you can play as Huckleberry Finn, too, if you're the second player.

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:09.720
I think, you know, that's a nice touch.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:12.060
Yeah, I read that. I didn't do that.

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:16.620
But. Is Huckleberry Finn, like, an imaginary friend of Tom Sawyer?

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:21.260
Does anybody know? I was just hoping we wouldn't talk about the book again so I didn't have to see me ignorant anymore.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:24.660
I haven't thought about the book since eighth grade and I kind of don't want to.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:26.200
So, Mike, you read it.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:32.080
Yeah, well, I did read it, but, you know, mostly I remember the painting the picket fences.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.980
I don't remember fighting the giant octopus in chapter one.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:39.760
Which is something that happens in the game.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:48.640
Yes. You know, but this is a platforming game, again, where you play as Tom Sawyer, and if you're a player, too, you play as Huckleberry Finn.

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:54.980
And you travel through just six levels, but they're pretty, you know, giant in size.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:03.340
And Tom Sawyer is, you know, from the cutscene that we get in the beginning of the game, he's in, like, school or something, and he's dreaming.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:11.100
And in this dream, he has to save, as Sean mentioned, Becky from Injun Joe, which I think I'm allowed to say because that's the character's name.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.340
I think you're allowed to say it.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.420
Yeah, yeah. I know it's not the best, but this is what we got to deal with.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:16.760
This is the material we've been given.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:22.560
And, yeah, so you're going to travel in this platforming game through six stages to get to rescue her.

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:27.760
I'm not quite sure what sets Tom Sawyer apart from other platforming games.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:37.180
So, Joe, Sean, is there anything specifically that you want to call out in regards to being or controlling Tom Sawyer?

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:39.800
I'll say that...

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:53.940
Yeah, go, Joe. I'll say I was pretty surprised, maybe just because of my, like, extremely low expectations for a game about The Adventures of Tom Sawyer, that it controls really nicely as a platformer on the NES.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:06:03.020
Like, it's kind of how, before we started this whole nostalgia experiment, kind of how I expected most NES games to control.

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:12.140
You know, it's no Super Mario Bros., but it feels good in a way that even, like, some games that we rate really highly don't feel as good to control, for the most part.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:15.820
It's not without its control, like, fluidity flaws.

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:18.420
But I don't know if you guys felt the same way.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:26.920
I think it's got a relatively intuitive, like, physics system.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:28.420
Like, obviously there's no physics system.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:33.320
But when I say that, I just mean, like, does the jump feel like something that you can do?

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:42.200
And it's not as expressive as a Mario game.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:45.800
But it at least, like, allows for some wiggle room.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:47.900
Like, there is some post-jump input.

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:51.460
But, like, this is all very, like, nitty-gritty.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:57.640
I don't think, like, the controls of this game were particularly great.

00:06:57.820 --> 00:06:59.680
But I think that's sort of what you're saying.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:01.060
It's just, like, it was passable.

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:10.540
Because that's really, like, all there is going on in terms of, like, the platforming levels of this game.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:14.980
And you're kind of like Mario with the Fire Flower at all times, right?

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:18.200
Like, you have this, I guess you're throwing rocks at first.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.180
And then you have the ability to get the slingshot.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:30.120
But unlike Mario's Fire Flower, the arc of these rock throws seems to be purposefully designed to go around the enemies.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.500
Unless you, like, hit them in the perfect spot.

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:36.080
There's something about the arch, you know, of the way they throw.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:41.260
That the enemy sprites are either, like, small enough that they'll go over them.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:47.120
Or they, like, fly in an area where it won't be able to quite hit them unless you jump in time to hit as well.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:51.160
I found that to be a little interesting of a control device.

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:54.140
Instead of squashing enemies like how you would in Super Mario Bros.,

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:59.140
you're stuck with just having your rock to throw at these guys.

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:03.820
The enemies just sort of appear very close to you, too, on screen.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:05.620
So there's not much of a warning.

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:08.900
You kind of have to, this is another one of those games where, and we've had these early on,

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:12.220
where you have to play a little cautiously if you want to live long,

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:17.640
just because you're not sure of the speed at which the enemies approach you once you do see them.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:22.520
Yeah, the first, my first continue, and it wasn't really a continue.

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:23.780
I just had to restart the whole game.

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:27.080
Lasted maybe, like, half of the first level.

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:28.280
I just kept dying.

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:33.640
But then I just realized, like, I have to play this game at a different pace than I'm used to.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:42.620
And I was able to, you know, pretty handily continue on as long as I was playing a little bit slower.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:51.520
Yeah, it's not, like, an overly difficult game, but it is, like, it does surprise you sometimes with the enemies.

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:55.940
Like, there's a lot of enemy variety, and a lot of them do different things.

00:08:56.020 --> 00:08:59.560
Like, even later in the game, suddenly an enemy will start doing something that you haven't seen done before.

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:01.820
Coming at you at a different angle or at a different speed,

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:05.280
or circling you in a way where you have to think about how you're going to catch them,

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:12.460
because, like you said, Mike, they're too close to you for you to hit them with the irregular rocks because of the arc of your throw.

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:25.520
Which could be seen as annoying and could also be seen as part of the game design of trying to really gauge how you're going to hit each of these enemies.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:27.500
And that would be a good thing, right?

00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:31.340
Like, if the enemies constantly change, I mean, we don't see that in every game, right?

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:35.400
A lot of times you're introduced to the different enemy types throughout the beginning of the game,

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:40.900
and then they either just get stronger or they have more health.

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:45.780
You know, like, either they do more damage to you or they have more health to retain more damage from you.

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:52.080
But in this game, the problem comes from the fact that there's a weird size to everything,

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:55.220
like, and a weird control to everything that you are.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:10:00.620
Like, with Tom going up and down ladders, there's this thing where, like, you almost think you're done climbing the ladder,

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:05.540
but if you aren't at the very tippy-tippy top, you fall all the way back down to the beginning.

00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:08.540
And I can't help but feel like that's the actual problem.

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:09.460
It's not the enemies.

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:15.780
It's the way you move within the space that makes everything a little extra challenging for this game.

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:22.960
And if there was a way to fix that, I don't know if it's, like, just making everything a little more zoomed out

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:29.100
so you can have more screen real estate to, you know, like, just so this way you can see yourself

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:32.440
and see what's coming at you from further away.

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:34.040
I think that's, like, what Super Mario Bros.

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:38.900
does, right? Like, you always see the Goombas just kind of slowly marching towards you.

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:40.860
They never just, like, pop up.

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:42.580
But there are things in Super Mario Bros.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:45.180
that pop up, like, say, piranha plants out of warp pipes.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:47.080
Like, so there just has to be a balance.

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:52.040
And this game didn't exactly figure out that balance and made it more just about

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:59.400
the actual traversal of the stages is the challenge rather than the types of enemies.

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:04.460
Yeah. Well, to sort of help kind of illustrate that, too, one thing I noticed,

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:09.980
if everybody's, I'm assuming, maybe not, but I'm assuming a lot of people in our audience

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:10.960
have played Super Mario Bros.

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:12.020
3. I would assume so.

00:11:12.020 --> 00:11:13.780
And if you remember in that game, they're the Cheap Cheaps.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:14.960
Or any Super Mario Bros.

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:15.900
games, but they're the Cheap Cheaps.

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:18.320
There are some levels, the Cheap Cheaps are the little fish that jump out of the water.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:21.800
There are some levels where they just, when you hit a certain spot on, you know,

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:25.780
the platform you're on, a fish will jump out of the water in your direction at you.

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:27.540
And it's pretty well designed.

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:31.180
Something very similar, almost exactly the same idea as in this game,

00:11:31.220 --> 00:11:32.580
a fish that jumps out of the water at you,

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:36.720
but it jumps out of the water literally, like, at the pixel you're already standing on

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:38.280
or that you're jumping towards.

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:44.700
And there's no avoiding it unless you, unless you, like, timidly jump a little bit forward

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:48.140
and then land back where you started, like, for every jump to see where it's coming from

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:49.220
because you don't know where they're coming from.

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:54.680
So it's like, it's like, yes, they had, like, that good, that good idea there,

00:11:54.820 --> 00:11:57.640
but it was implemented in a way where it's, like, just not player friendly.

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:01.760
It makes it so, like, avoiding it is possible, but not fun.

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:03.780
It's just, like, a chore to, like, test every step.

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:08.500
You know, that's one little tiny part of this game, but, you know,

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:12.000
I think that that really is where I felt the most of what you're talking about.

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:19.760
Yeah, I get, so one thing about the enemies in this game is a lot of them are based on player input,

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:23.880
and I don't really know if it's, like, a one-to-one thing,

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:30.960
but even in the first level, one of the standard mobs is, like, a pirate dude,

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:36.720
and it will react to when you throw a rock, and it will dodge the rock,

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:38.220
and part of that is cool.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:44.720
Like, it's cool to see that kind of dynamic response to you,

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:49.320
and it's not just, like, an enemy that's walking towards you single-mindedly,

00:12:50.140 --> 00:12:55.680
but other parts of that, it's just kind of annoying because it feels cheap

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:57.200
because it's just reading your inputs,

00:12:57.220 --> 00:13:01.180
and I don't know if it's good or bad,

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:05.220
but there were those aspects to these enemies, it seemed,

00:13:06.220 --> 00:13:10.580
in the standard levels and even in the raft levels,

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:14.300
so it's kind of a mixed bag.

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:18.440
Yeah, I had mixed feelings about that specific enemy, too,

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:21.980
but the part of me that liked it just to play Devil's Advocate

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:25.900
was because it does make, you know,

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:28.160
it feels different than all the other enemies that you're running into,

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:32.200
and it makes, like, that enemy encounter feel like a moment every time.

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:35.100
You know, it makes it feel like, okay, I've got to think about how I can,

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:36.680
you know, he's going to jump, so I've got to jump,

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:39.320
or I've got to wait until he lands and be far enough away from him.

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:41.920
So, like, I like that element of it,

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:45.380
but, yeah, it is sort of, I don't know, counterintuitive at times.

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:00.280
And, Sean, you mentioned the raft stage,

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:03.480
which, you know, that's not a platforming thing.

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:06.480
The game almost becomes a shmup where you now throw your rocks.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:08.580
Almost, yeah. Like, guns, you know?

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:11.020
Like, you're shooting your rocks out of a gun or something,

00:14:11.060 --> 00:14:12.540
and you're going on this raft,

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:14.720
and there's something really interesting here, though,

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:18.920
with the level design where, as Tom Sawyer, you have this,

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:20.500
you know, who cares if it's unrealistic or not?

00:14:20.520 --> 00:14:25.380
You have this very large jump that doesn't sync with the raft below you,

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:28.860
but you still have freedom of your jump in the air

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:32.900
so that even if your raft is being, like, pulled in a specific direction

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:34.700
or you're not quite lined up with it,

00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:36.880
you still get a chance to do that.

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:40.100
To correct, yeah. And I almost wonder, like, that's a game in itself,

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:42.880
and it's only used for one level.

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:44.840
Like, it's never called back to.

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:48.700
There's no other additional, like, bonus modes in this game.

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:50.060
Why do you think they did that?

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:53.980
So that's one of the most interesting parts of this game

00:14:53.980 --> 00:14:56.420
is that because, like, by all measures,

00:14:56.700 --> 00:15:01.300
this, I don't know if we've really made it clear

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:02.940
with how we're talking about it so far,

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:06.460
but, like, a lot of this game is kind of ho-hum,

00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:09.880
like, oh, this is just one of those 700 games in the NES.

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:13.060
But you can tell that they're trying things,

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:20.380
whether it's the enemy design or the platforming

00:15:20.380 --> 00:15:26.940
or this sort of this raft game design.

00:15:27.460 --> 00:15:29.000
They are trying things.

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:34.160
And the fact that they had it so that you are part of this,

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:36.360
you're riding this raft and you have to jump.

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:38.920
Like, there are parts that you just have to.

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:42.020
And it's sort of like a complex,

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:47.380
a relatively complex mechanic

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:51.540
where it's going to just continue with momentum

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:54.400
of however you left the raft going when you jumped

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:57.480
and you now have to avoid whatever obstacle

00:15:57.480 --> 00:16:01.720
you were trying to avoid while also landing on the raft.

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:05.280
And that is engaging and that is cool,

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:08.460
but it just happened so infrequently.

00:16:08.460 --> 00:16:10.680
It was just that one level for the most part

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:12.360
that you have to worry about it.

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:16.420
But, like, that was probably one of the most engaging

00:16:16.420 --> 00:16:18.800
parts of the game for me was, like,

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:24.100
now I have to do these things at the same time.

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:27.960
I don't really know how to describe it beyond that,

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:29.800
but I like that.

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:31.620
Do you guys like that?

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:35.220
Yeah, I thought that was a great, you know,

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:38.820
and I thought a similar thing about some of the other levels as well,

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:42.240
but not to the extent of this raft level,

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:45.640
that it was a great, like, format change

00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:47.700
and it was something we haven't seen before.

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:49.140
It made me think differently about it.

00:16:49.220 --> 00:16:50.580
And it was pretty fun.

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:52.360
And, you know, I got to say,

00:16:52.460 --> 00:16:55.360
I think that your description of it being

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:58.460
just another one of the 700 games on the NES

00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:01.600
is, like, the perfect way to articulate what this game is for me.

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:05.120
But I will say, with things like this raft thing,

00:17:05.180 --> 00:17:07.840
I think it blends into all, like, yeah,

00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:09.660
that list of all those other games like that,

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:14.160
but it does feel, at least to me, above average

00:17:14.580 --> 00:17:20.020
because of its ambition and, like, trying new things

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:21.040
and not just getting, like,

00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:24.940
not just getting, doing the same thing over and over again,

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:26.200
adding, like, a raft level.

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:27.440
Yes, I would have liked more of those,

00:17:27.560 --> 00:17:30.340
but having the enemy variety and all that stuff,

00:17:30.460 --> 00:17:31.760
it was kind of cool to me.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:33.760
I mean, yeah, but I do think there's a difference, though,

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:36.760
between, like, a game trying a lot of different things

00:17:36.760 --> 00:17:38.680
or feeling of progression

00:17:38.680 --> 00:17:42.540
and then also, like, making sure that those things work well.

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:46.400
So I feel like a lot of the things that this game introduces

00:17:47.740 --> 00:17:50.580
either don't matter because they can be cheesed

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:56.160
or they become frustrating because of their design development.

00:17:56.380 --> 00:18:01.100
Oh, absolutely. Yeah, you know, the boss fights are something that's, like...

00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:03.260
I accidentally cheesed these boss fights.

00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:05.540
Right, right. You don't even know you're doing it,

00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:07.460
but, like, that octopus one we keep talking about,

00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:09.460
it's just the first boss. There's other cool bosses, too,

00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:12.540
like a giant water dragon and a gorilla.

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:15.840
But, like, the octopus, just as an example,

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:18.160
if you just constantly fire at it,

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:20.480
before it ever shoots its first attack at you,

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:21.480
it will die.

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:23.740
That thing attacks?

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:24.960
I didn't even know it attacks.

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:27.900
I thought it was, like, teaching you what boss battles are.

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:29.300
I didn't even know I had an attack.

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:30.860
But that could have been an exciting thing.

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:32.820
That could have been an exciting moment for the game

00:18:32.820 --> 00:18:34.600
and a nice boss fight where you have to, like,

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:38.100
dodge the tentacles or something and hit the weak spots,

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:39.340
but it doesn't do that.

00:18:39.380 --> 00:18:41.640
And yet there are other elements of this game

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:45.020
that, whether through, like, platform trickery

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:49.020
or through, you know, the way enemies appear on screen,

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:52.500
it becomes a harder game than it has to be.

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:55.440
It's almost like the game isn't balanced in a way

00:18:55.440 --> 00:18:58.120
where, like, any particular level of this game

00:18:58.120 --> 00:18:58.940
isn't challenging,

00:18:58.940 --> 00:19:02.080
but there are challenging moments in each level

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:03.500
that just become confusing.

00:19:03.760 --> 00:19:05.380
Yeah. Like, wait, why is this the hard part?

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:08.000
Yeah, and, like, when you say that there's a difference

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:11.450
between seeing interesting ideas

00:19:12.200 --> 00:19:15.540
or new ideas and how they execute,

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:19.180
like, that's basically, like, that's the story of this game

00:19:19.180 --> 00:19:20.900
is that, like, I can tell

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:22.510
they're trying to do something.

00:19:23.480 --> 00:19:24.580
Something that isn't, like,

00:19:24.620 --> 00:19:26.160
because this is a Thomas Sawyer game,

00:19:26.300 --> 00:19:28.260
and no, it's not, like, a big franchise.

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:30.480
Like, they're just making a game

00:19:30.480 --> 00:19:32.280
and trying to make parts of it unique.

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:36.720
So I see that, and I give them credit for that.

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:38.680
But, like, yeah, it's obviously,

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:40.300
the execution is flawed.

00:19:40.560 --> 00:19:43.160
And I think most games

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:45.060
that aren't just straight cash grabs,

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:47.540
we can see something like that.

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:50.160
And it's just odd seeing it

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:53.660
in something that is so,

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:57.640
what I imagine is so, like, laser-focused

00:19:57.640 --> 00:19:59.680
and just, like, this is just a game

00:19:59.680 --> 00:20:01.120
we're going to try and sell to parents

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:03.740
as, like, an edutainment thing,

00:20:03.740 --> 00:20:05.660
and then the game is just nonsense

00:20:05.660 --> 00:20:08.360
about dragons and throwing rocks.

00:20:08.380 --> 00:20:10.500
Like, it just, like,

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:14.000
it illustrates the history

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:16.160
of NES shovelware.

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:17.780
And I'm not saying this game is shovelware,

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:19.920
but it may as well be with how...

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:21.280
Yeah, no. Yeah. Go ahead.

00:20:21.280 --> 00:20:22.620
I get exactly what you're saying

00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:25.080
because it's almost like,

00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:26.480
you know, the assignment

00:20:26.480 --> 00:20:29.160
from the publisher was clear, right?

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:30.400
Like, oh, we want to take

00:20:30.400 --> 00:20:32.620
the Tom Sawyer, like, book

00:20:32.620 --> 00:20:34.040
and make it into a video game.

00:20:34.180 --> 00:20:35.860
And then you just get a couple of guys

00:20:35.860 --> 00:20:37.500
who know how to put together a game,

00:20:37.700 --> 00:20:39.380
almost like tag team in, you know,

00:20:39.420 --> 00:20:41.780
be like, all right, cool. You did your 30 minutes of programming.

00:20:42.080 --> 00:20:43.100
You're in, Brian. Now it's my turn. Yeah.

00:20:43.420 --> 00:20:44.480
Right. And you just, like,

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:46.080
take either the elements or whatever.

00:20:46.300 --> 00:20:47.700
And, Joe, I'm sure you'll disagree,

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:49.580
but I don't mean that, like, 100%.

00:20:49.580 --> 00:20:50.920
I just think, like, at times,

00:20:51.720 --> 00:20:53.280
not that the game feels bipolar,

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:55.380
but it feels uneven

00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:56.800
in its own difficulty.

00:20:56.800 --> 00:20:58.300
There are times in this game,

00:20:58.500 --> 00:20:59.620
even in later levels,

00:20:59.900 --> 00:21:02.080
where you could just avoid everything

00:21:02.080 --> 00:21:03.640
and keep making forward progress

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:05.240
and you have, you know,

00:21:05.300 --> 00:21:07.280
you only have, like, your health as it is.

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:08.640
Like, there's no extra health bars

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:09.480
or anything to cheese it,

00:21:09.540 --> 00:21:11.800
but you don't have to make contact with much.

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:13.240
And then there's other moments in this game

00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:15.000
where all of a sudden

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:16.060
there's, like, eight enemies

00:21:16.060 --> 00:21:19.100
and now you're flying on a balloon

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:20.960
and you have to dodge thunderclouds

00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:23.180
that are shooting, you know,

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:24.800
bolts of lightning at you.

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:27.580
And it's just, like, when did this take over?

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:30.240
Like, I didn't expect this to just happen.

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:32.260
Well, so I don't fully disagree,

00:21:32.260 --> 00:21:35.160
but it does seem like I have a higher opinion

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:37.400
on the game overall than you guys might.

00:21:38.360 --> 00:21:41.080
But I'll say that, like, for what's...

00:21:41.080 --> 00:21:43.040
I think part of it is because of my expectations

00:21:43.040 --> 00:21:44.780
going into it, because of what you're talking about,

00:21:44.820 --> 00:21:46.740
this being a Tom Sawyer game

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:47.780
that someone just said, like,

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:49.460
okay, let's just try and see

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:52.120
if we can get this out there.

00:21:52.120 --> 00:21:54.160
But to me, it seems clear that, like,

00:21:54.360 --> 00:21:55.680
almost against the odds,

00:21:55.960 --> 00:21:57.720
somebody who developed this game

00:21:57.720 --> 00:21:59.080
really cared about it.

00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:01.860
Like, we've had so many games on the NES

00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:03.600
that just kind of, like, okay, they just put it out there.

00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:04.940
This feels like someone was, like,

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:06.980
oh, I really want... Like, someone had some ideas

00:22:06.980 --> 00:22:08.500
and someone was passionate about it,

00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:10.280
which is what surprised me the most.

00:22:10.400 --> 00:22:11.280
Like, it feels like

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:13.980
they put the effort

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:16.100
into making the game feel good.

00:22:16.100 --> 00:22:17.960
They put the effort into making this enemy variety.

00:22:18.100 --> 00:22:19.020
I mean, literally every stage

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:20.720
has, like, totally different enemies

00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:21.880
and totally different, like,

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:24.540
ways about it. Structure, yeah. And there's no stage

00:22:24.540 --> 00:22:25.920
in particular to me

00:22:25.920 --> 00:22:28.060
that feels bad

00:22:28.060 --> 00:22:29.200
or just thrown in there.

00:22:29.240 --> 00:22:31.020
I think that, you know,

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:33.080
the flaws that we're talking about,

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:35.240
like, they just come up

00:22:35.240 --> 00:22:36.140
throughout a little bit.

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:37.540
And I even think some of them,

00:22:37.660 --> 00:22:39.160
I mean, I think I have a higher opinion

00:22:39.160 --> 00:22:40.720
on some of these things we're calling flaws

00:22:40.720 --> 00:22:41.820
than you guys do, too,

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:44.060
because I didn't hate

00:22:44.060 --> 00:22:45.300
the enemy placement

00:22:45.300 --> 00:22:46.640
other than really that one...

00:22:46.640 --> 00:22:47.620
The only one I could think of

00:22:47.620 --> 00:22:48.540
is the one that I mentioned

00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:49.480
with the fish.

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:51.700
Like, some of it was definitely not, like...

00:22:51.700 --> 00:22:52.640
No, no, I don't hate

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:53.460
Perfect game design,

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:54.480
but, like...

00:22:54.480 --> 00:22:56.900
What's that? I don't hate the enemy placement.

00:22:57.200 --> 00:22:59.720
It was more so just, like...

00:22:59.720 --> 00:23:00.880
No. I'm sorry.

00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:02.180
Finish your thought. That's all right.

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:03.920
Placement was maybe the wrong word,

00:23:03.940 --> 00:23:05.220
but with what you guys are talking about

00:23:05.220 --> 00:23:07.980
where it's, like, yeah, the AI being

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:09.800
a little cheap sometimes

00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:11.920
or, like, I know that there are times

00:23:11.920 --> 00:23:13.040
where, like, an enemy just comes at you

00:23:13.040 --> 00:23:14.240
and, like, if you didn't think

00:23:14.240 --> 00:23:15.180
about it ahead of time,

00:23:15.540 --> 00:23:16.460
you're kind of screwed,

00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:18.640
which would suck

00:23:18.640 --> 00:23:19.540
if it didn't, like...

00:23:19.540 --> 00:23:20.660
I felt like I sort of

00:23:20.660 --> 00:23:21.580
learned that stuff

00:23:21.580 --> 00:23:23.400
pretty early on whenever I met a new enemy

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:24.460
and then when I saw them again,

00:23:24.680 --> 00:23:26.700
I at least had the knowledge

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:28.420
to, like, figure out, like,

00:23:28.500 --> 00:23:29.460
what I should have done

00:23:29.460 --> 00:23:31.400
even if sometimes I didn't do it and died.

00:23:31.580 --> 00:23:32.820
I could still be like, well, okay,

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:34.000
I guess I screwed that up

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:35.000
and I should have done something else.

00:23:36.220 --> 00:23:38.400
I'm not fully in that camp either.

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:40.460
Like, I definitely think

00:23:40.460 --> 00:23:42.400
that that element of this game

00:23:43.300 --> 00:23:45.260
could have been buttoned up and improved,

00:23:45.260 --> 00:23:46.880
but it just didn't, like...

00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:48.200
It was nowhere near as bad

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:49.600
as some other games that we've had

00:23:49.600 --> 00:23:50.840
where, like...

00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:53.000
I think it was, like, was it the Karate Kid

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:54.260
or one of those games?

00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:56.160
Yeah, Karate Kid has a lot of problems

00:23:56.160 --> 00:23:57.200
that I think, you know,

00:23:57.260 --> 00:23:58.780
you're right. We're saying this game

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:00.200
has those problems. It doesn't have that

00:24:00.200 --> 00:24:02.200
on that scale, but it is worth highlighting

00:24:02.200 --> 00:24:03.320
that there are some issues.

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:04.740
Yeah, but I don't think

00:24:04.740 --> 00:24:06.120
that I've been criticizing

00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:07.540
this game's enemy placement.

00:24:07.860 --> 00:24:09.280
I think I've... I mean,

00:24:09.620 --> 00:24:11.140
what I've been saying about this game

00:24:11.140 --> 00:24:12.960
is that a lot of it

00:24:12.960 --> 00:24:15.760
is cookie cutter, except for a lot of it,

00:24:15.880 --> 00:24:17.480
but it's all...

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:19.160
None of it really

00:24:20.420 --> 00:24:21.360
makes it feel

00:24:21.360 --> 00:24:22.700
like a cohesive piece.

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:23.840
It's just like,

00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:25.220
oh, it does this thing

00:24:25.220 --> 00:24:27.040
and that's new, but it only does that

00:24:27.040 --> 00:24:28.280
for a level. And like, oh,

00:24:28.320 --> 00:24:29.860
these enemies, like,

00:24:30.040 --> 00:24:31.260
respond to you in a way.

00:24:31.480 --> 00:24:32.960
And sometimes it feels cheap,

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:35.700
but otherwise, it's actually like...

00:24:36.600 --> 00:24:37.860
It's kind of an interesting way

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:41.960
to build some enemy behaviors,

00:24:43.160 --> 00:24:44.840
but it's only a few of them.

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:46.980
And so a lot of it

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:48.640
is just like, oh, it did this well,

00:24:48.760 --> 00:24:51.120
but it was just...

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:52.340
It's just one piece

00:24:52.340 --> 00:24:53.640
of an otherwise,

00:24:53.640 --> 00:24:54.840
like, un...

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:56.320
And I'm kind of like

00:24:57.620 --> 00:24:58.820
telegraphing my opinion,

00:24:59.060 --> 00:25:00.040
but yeah. You don't take what you've learned

00:25:00.040 --> 00:25:02.520
and then have to improve it

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:03.860
in the next level where it throws, like,

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:05.260
the harder version of that

00:25:05.260 --> 00:25:06.440
at you or anything like that.

00:25:06.440 --> 00:25:07.700
Like, it's just like...

00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:09.100
It's just like six

00:25:10.060 --> 00:25:11.220
totally different things.

00:25:11.740 --> 00:25:13.700
And like, I was going to save this

00:25:13.700 --> 00:25:14.760
for my essential vote,

00:25:14.840 --> 00:25:15.780
but maybe this will like

00:25:15.780 --> 00:25:16.900
help clear up

00:25:16.900 --> 00:25:17.940
kind of how I'm feeling about it.

00:25:18.020 --> 00:25:19.180
Like, by the end of this,

00:25:19.300 --> 00:25:20.260
I felt personally like

00:25:20.260 --> 00:25:21.840
this was the first six levels

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:23.100
of what would have been

00:25:23.100 --> 00:25:24.180
a pretty good game.

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:27.220
Like, I felt like it was like...

00:25:27.220 --> 00:25:28.120
I wanted more of this.

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:29.480
I wanted like more levels

00:25:29.480 --> 00:25:30.400
like this later.

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:32.720
Like, you know, like, I felt like it was just like

00:25:32.720 --> 00:25:34.120
they had a bunch of ideas

00:25:34.120 --> 00:25:35.220
and they just put them all in here

00:25:35.220 --> 00:25:36.660
with no, like...

00:25:36.660 --> 00:25:37.540
Yeah, no through line.

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:39.560
Yeah. That is like...

00:25:39.560 --> 00:25:41.000
We haven't really touched upon it,

00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:41.940
but for something

00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:43.000
that's supposed to be based off

00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:44.080
of a literary,

00:25:44.900 --> 00:25:48.020
historic, like, through line

00:25:48.020 --> 00:25:49.300
of American culture,

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:52.040
this makes no sense.

00:25:52.400 --> 00:25:54.960
And I think even

00:25:54.960 --> 00:25:57.360
if I knew Tom Sawyer,

00:25:57.480 --> 00:25:58.920
this would make no sense.

00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:00.460
Does it make sense?

00:26:00.600 --> 00:26:01.980
Did anybody read this?

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:03.380
We didn't... Well, I mean,

00:26:03.440 --> 00:26:04.300
I didn't read the manual,

00:26:04.540 --> 00:26:05.360
but I'll tell you,

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:07.400
like, you know, just from playing the game.

00:26:07.560 --> 00:26:09.580
No, it is a nonsensical adventure here

